## 0. Purpose

Phase 06D converts the frozen GrowFlow design-token system into a reusable, implementation-grade component system.

The objective is to establish **one component language** that every public page, authenticated workplace, workflow, dashboard, document surface, AI interface, and administrative interface can consume.

This phase defines:

- component taxonomy
- component responsibilities
- component anatomy
- variants
- sizes
- states
- interaction behavior
- accessibility behavior
- responsive behavior
- composition rules
- data behavior
- AI behavior
- loading/error/empty behavior
- implementation boundaries
- component ownership
- naming conventions
- testing requirements
- Storybook/design-system expectations
- Stitch mapping
- prohibited patterns
- Definition of Done

This document does **not** define individual product pages. Page-specific composition belongs to Phase 06I and later page specifications.

---

# 1. Component-System Philosophy

GrowFlow follows:

```text
Tokens
  ↓
Primitive Components
  ↓
Composite Components
  ↓
Domain Components
  ↓
Page Composition
```

The component system must make correct design the easiest implementation path.

A developer should normally be able to construct a page from existing components instead of creating one-off UI.

---

# 2. Core Principles

## 2.1 Reuse Before Reinvention

Before creating a new component:

1. Search the component system.
2. Check whether an existing component supports the need.
3. Check whether a variant is sufficient.
4. Check whether composition solves the problem.
5. Only then propose a new component.

## 2.2 Components Have Responsibilities

A component should have one clear responsibility.

Avoid components that simultaneously own:

- routing
- API requests
- business mutations
- visual rendering
- authorization logic
- workflow orchestration

Domain containers may coordinate these concerns, but visual components should remain reusable.

## 2.3 Components Are Not Pages

A component:

- renders a reusable UI concept
- exposes controlled props/state
- does not own arbitrary page layout
- does not silently fetch unrelated data

A page:

- owns route context
- composes components
- coordinates page data
- handles page-level workflow state

