# 18. IconButton

For icon-only actions.

Required:

- accessible label
- tooltip where useful
- minimum hit area
- focus state
- disabled state

IconButton must not be used when the action meaning cannot be understood from an icon.

---

# 19. ButtonGroup

Groups related actions.

Supports:

```text
horizontal
vertical
compact
standard
```

Use sparingly.

Destructive actions should not be visually ambiguous with ordinary actions.

---

# 20. Toggle

Represents a reversible on/off choice.

Use only when the user understands the state as a binary setting.

Do not use Toggle where an immediate action button is more appropriate.

---

# 21. Checkbox

Used for independent multi-select choices.

Must support:

```text
unchecked
checked
indeterminate
disabled
focus
error
```

Indeterminate state is essential for hierarchical selection.

---

# 22. Radio

Used for mutually exclusive choices.

Do not use radio groups for large searchable collections; use Combobox/select patterns instead.

---

# 23. Switch

Used primarily for settings or persistent enable/disable behavior.

Difference from Toggle:

- Switch communicates persistent setting state.
- Toggle may communicate local interaction state.

---

# 24. Select

Used for bounded options.

Required:

- label
- selected value
- placeholder where needed
- disabled
- error
- loading where options are asynchronous
- keyboard interaction

---

# 25. Combobox

Used for:

- search + selection
- large option lists
- people selection
- project selection
- resource selection

Must support:

- keyboard navigation
- filtering
- selected state
- loading
- no results
- error
- clear action where appropriate

---

# 26. Segmented Control

Used for switching between closely related display modes.

Examples:

```text
List | Board | Timeline
```

Do not use as primary application navigation.

---

# 27. Tabs

Tabs represent sibling views within one resource context.

Rules:

- preserve route context where tabs are route-addressable
- selected state must be clear
- keyboard navigation required
- avoid excessive tab counts
- mobile may scroll or transform presentation

Tabs must not replace unrelated global navigation.

---

# 28. Menu / Dropdown

Used for compact action collections.

Must support:

- keyboard navigation
- focus management
- escape-to-close
- outside interaction
- disabled items
- destructive item styling where appropriate

