# 109. Component Composition Rules

Prefer composition:

```text
Card
 ├── Header
 ├── Body
 └── Footer
```

over giant configuration components with dozens of unrelated props.

Prefer:

```text
Table
 ├── TableHeader
 ├── TableBody
 └── TableRow
```

when advanced control is needed.

---

# 110. Props and API Design

Component APIs should:

- be explicit
- be typed
- avoid boolean-prop explosion
- use semantic variants
- provide controlled/uncontrolled patterns intentionally
- avoid exposing implementation details

Bad:

```text
<Button green rounded2 shadow3 special />
```

Good:

```text
<Button variant="primary" size="standard" />
```

---

# 111. Controlled vs Uncontrolled

Use controlled state when:

- parent/application state matters
- URL state matters
- server state matters
- workflow state matters

Use uncontrolled state when:

- interaction is purely local
- state does not need external synchronization

---

# 112. Data Fetching Boundary

Presentational components should not directly call backend APIs unless they are explicitly designated data-aware domain containers.

Preferred:

```text
Page
 ↓
Feature/Data Layer
 ↓
Domain Component
 ↓
Presentational Components
```

---

# 113. Authorization Boundary

Components may receive authorization-aware capabilities.

They must not be the security boundary.

Correct:

```text
Backend authorization
    ↓
API response/capability
    ↓
Frontend rendering
```

Incorrect:

```text
Hidden button = security
```

---

# 114. Routing Boundary

Reusable components should not invent routes.

Pages/domain containers provide canonical navigation destinations.

Navigation components may consume route objects or URLs.

---

# 115. Server State Boundary

Server state includes:

- project data
- tasks
- milestones
- risks
- documents
- users
- groups
- AI execution records

Components render server state but do not become the canonical store.

---

# 116. UI State Boundary

UI state includes:

- modal open
- filter expansion
- selected tab
- expanded row
- temporary form values

Keep local unless broader coordination is required.

---

# 117. Streaming State Boundary

Streaming state includes:

- SSE connection
- AI progress
- live execution updates
- notification delivery
- processing updates

Components subscribe to streaming state through the frontend state architecture.

---

# 118. Event Reconciliation

After streaming events:

```text
SSE
 ↓
validate event
 ↓
update streaming state
 ↓
invalidate/reconcile server state
 ↓
render
```

Components must not assume SSE is the canonical source of truth.

---

# 119. Optimistic UI

Use optimistic updates only when:

- failure is easy to recover
- result is predictable
- rollback is well-defined
- security/authorization does not depend on optimistic state

Prefer confirmed server state for:

- project lifecycle
- permissions
- AI execution
- blueprint persistence
- project changes
- high-impact mutations

---

# 120. Component Error Boundaries

Recommended hierarchy:

```text
Application
  ↓
Role
  ↓
Feature
  ↓
Page
  ↓
Component
```

A failure in one component should not unnecessarily destroy the entire application.

---

# 121. Performance Requirements

Components should:

- avoid unnecessary rerenders
- virtualize very large lists
- lazy-load heavy visualizations
- avoid shipping unnecessary dependencies
- avoid excessive DOM depth
- avoid perpetual animation
- clean up subscriptions

---

# 122. SSE-Aware Components

SSE-consuming components must:

- subscribe only when relevant
- unsubscribe when unmounted
- tolerate reconnects
- tolerate duplicate events
- reconcile server state
- expose meaningful disconnected state when user action is affected

---

# 123. AI Component Performance

AI components must support:

- streaming updates
- long responses
- cancellation
- reconnection
- partial rendering
- final reconciliation
- failure recovery

Avoid rendering thousands of independent nodes for long AI output when a more efficient representation is possible.

---

# 124. Security Requirements

Components must not expose:

- API keys
- provider credentials
- internal secrets
- unauthorized project context
- hidden administrative data

Sensitive information should be controlled by backend authorization and response shaping.

---

# 125. Component Naming Convention

Recommended:

```text
PascalCase
```

Examples:

```text
Button
DataTable
ProjectHeader
AIExecutionIndicator
WorkflowStepper
```

Use domain prefixes only when they improve clarity.

---

# 126. File Organization

Recommended:

```text
frontend/
└── design-system/
    ├── foundations/
    ├── controls/
    ├── forms/
    ├── navigation/
    ├── feedback/
    ├── overlays/
    ├── data/
    ├── dashboards/
    ├── documents/
    ├── ai/
    ├── workflow/
    ├── project/
    ├── people/
    └── system/
```

Feature-specific components that are not reusable belong in the feature layer rather than the global design system.

